														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2120416">
									

<p>The more I read this, the less funny it becomes.<br />
The first time around, I found myself positively rolling with laughter. Bootleg humans being treated like defective Apple products is a hilarious concept, and the &quot;reboot&quot; won me over.<br />
On the second read, however, it had lost some of its original charm because I had read it previously, and the flaws started to make themselves apparent.<br />
Biggest issue right off the bat is something Kalinin has referred to as &quot;unknowable eldritch horror which talks like Dave from Accounting.&quot; This entity lives in an entirely different dimension to us, and that has undoubtedly shaped their lingo and conversational lexicon. To have this thing speak like you or I when we phone in to Apple to complain about our products makes this more relatable and therefore more humorous, but ultimately detracts from the article for me. Yes, you did go with the whole &quot;replace 'God' in 'goddammit' with some other name&quot; to make him sound more alien, but honestly, I feel that that trope has been exhausted to the point where seeing it just makes me cringe a bit (bonus points for making the deity's name have a bunch of Z's and X's.)<br />
I'm not implying that you have to change anything, as it seems that most people are fine with it, but I'm just saying that that particular issue irks me a lot. I know that the actual entity isn't the focus of the article; the company and its products are, but it still annoyed me quite a lot.<br />
Second one is probably just me being dense, but how has the Foundation not located this company yet? If we know that the company is located in the southwestern United States, couldn't we just comb through each suspicious company till we found it? I mean, I know it's a bit meticulous, but this is the Foundation we're talking about, and considering this is a Euclid-class object, I'd assume that they'd go to the trouble of doing it anyway. How did we even manage to trace them to the southwestern US in the first place?<br />
Anyway, I still find this pretty amusing, despite the personal quibbles I had with it, so it balances out to a novote.</p>

